    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Darwin argued that species were not separately created but arose by common descent in a branching pattern, with natural selection as the chief agent of change.</li><li>The book was an abstract hurried into print after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace">Alfred Russel Wallace</a> sent Darwin an essay describing a similar mechanism, and it went through six editions in Darwin's lifetime.</li><li>Within two decades most scientists accepted evolution, but natural selection became central to evolutionary theory only with the modern evolutionary synthesis of the 1930s and 1940s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> of 1250 copies was offered to booksellers on 22 November 1859, and all available copies were taken up at once; Mudie's Library alone took 500.</p></aside>
    </section>

