    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1838</span><span class="rf-text">Darwin reads Thomas Malthus on population and sees how selection among varieties could work in nature</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1844</span><span class="rf-text">Darwin expands his sketch of his theory into a 230-page Essay, to be published if he died prematurely</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1858</span><span class="rf-text">Papers by Wallace and Darwin are read at the Linnean Society, and Darwin begins his abstract on 20 July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1859</span><span class="rf-text">On the Origin of Species is published by John Murray on 24 November</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1860</span><span class="rf-text">The second edition adds the phrase by the Creator to the closing sentence, and Appleton's issues the American edition</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1872</span><span class="rf-text">The sixth edition appears as The Origin of Species at half the price, after Darwin answers George Jackson Mivart</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1930s</span><span class="rf-text">The modern evolutionary synthesis makes natural selection central to evolutionary theory</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-lamarck"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-lamarck.jpg" alt="Jean-Baptiste Lamarck" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-lamarck">Jean-Baptiste Lamarck</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Baptiste_de_Lamarck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charles Thévenin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Evolutionary ideas existed before Darwin. In the 1790s his grandfather Erasmus Darwin outlined a hypothesis of the transmutation of species, and in 1809 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-lamarck">Jean-Baptiste Lamarck</a> published a more developed theory in which organisms adapted by inheriting changes caused by use or disuse. Such ideas were controversial because they conflicted with the beliefs that species were unchanging parts of a designed hierarchy and that humans were unique. In the first half of the 19th century the English scientific establishment was closely tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, and science was part of natural theology.</p><p>Darwin studied medicine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> from 1825, then learnt science as natural theology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>. In December 1831 he joined the Beagle expedition as a gentleman naturalist and geologist, and in March 1837 the ornithologist John Gould told him that mockingbirds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galapagos-islands">Galápagos Islands</a> represented three separate species. Darwin began to speculate in notebooks that one species might change into another. In late September 1838 he read Malthus, and by December he had noted a similarity between breeders selecting traits and nature selecting among variants.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;On the Origin of Species&#8221; cites where it covers Developments before Darwin's theory, Inception of Darwin's theory, Further development:</p><ul><li>Peter J. Bowler, Evolution: The History of an Idea (University of California Press, 2003)</li><li>Charles Darwin, The Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection, or the Preservation of Favoured Races in the Struggle for Life (John Murray, 1872)</li><li>Richard Keynes (ed.), Charles Darwin's Zoology Notes &amp; Specimen Lists from HMS Beagle (Cambridge University Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The book argues that species had not been separately created and that natural selection had been the chief agent of change. Ernst Mayr later summarised the argument as facts and inferences: species are fertile enough that populations would grow if all offspring survived, yet populations stay roughly stable; resources are limited, so a struggle for survival follows; individuals vary and much of the variation is heritable; those better suited to the environment leave more offspring. The accumulation of such variations produces new species.</p><p>The first four chapters compare selection in nature with the selection practised by breeders, using fancy pigeons, all descended from the rock dove, as the main illustration. Later chapters take up difficulties for the theory, including the rarity of transitional forms, the origin of complex organs such as the eye, instincts such as the cell-building of honey bees, and the imperfection of the geological record. Further chapters set out evidence from the distribution of species, from classification, from homologous structures such as the hand of a man and the wing of a bat, from embryos and from rudimentary organs. The final chapter ends with the passage on an entangled bank and a single sentence saying that light will be thrown on the origin of man and his history.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;On the Origin of Species&#8221; cites where it covers Summary of Darwin's theory, Title pages and introduction, Variation under domestication and under nature, Struggle for existence, natural selection, and divergence:</p><ul><li>Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection, or the Preservation of Favoured Races in the Struggle for Life (John Murray, 1859)</li><li>Charles Darwin, The Annotated Origin: A Facsimile of the First Edition of On the Origin of Species Annotated by James T. Costa (Belknap Press of Harvard University Press, 2009)</li><li>Charles Darwin, The Descent of Man, and Selection in Relation to Sex (John Murray, 1871)</li><li>Peter J. Bowler, Evolution: The History of an Idea (University of California Press, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace.jpg" alt="Alfred Russel Wallace" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace">Alfred Russel Wallace</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred-Russel-Wallace-c1895.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">London Stereoscopic and Photographic Company (active 1855-1922), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Darwin had the framework of his theory by 1838 but published it only in 1859. He roughed out a 35-page Pencil Sketch in June 1842 and a 230-page Essay by July 1844, then spent eight years on barnacles and began working full time on evolution in 1854. By 1856 his theory had a mass of supporting evidence. In May 1856, after Charles Lyell urged him to publish to establish priority, he began a sketch that grew into a planned big book on Natural Selection. Biographers have proposed various personal reasons for the delay, while the historian John van Wyhe has found that the idea of a delay dates only from the 1940s.</p><p>On 18 June 1858 Darwin received from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace">Alfred Russel Wallace</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a>, twenty pages describing a mechanism similar to his own. Lyell and Joseph Dalton Hooker arranged a joint presentation at the Linnean Society on 1 July 1858, which drew little reaction. Darwin then began an abstract of his whole work on 20 July. John Murray agreed in March 1859 to publish it on the terms he gave Lyell, and objected to the word abstract in the title. After a draft title suggested On the Mutability of Species, the title was settled with Murray's persuasion as On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-henry-huxley"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-henry-huxley.jpg" alt="Thomas Henry Huxley" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-henry-huxley">Thomas Henry Huxley</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:T.H.Huxley(Woodburytype).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lock &amp; Whitfield, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The book was written for non-specialist readers and attracted widespread interest. Much of the early reaction was hostile, in large part because few reviewers understood the theory, but Darwin was already respected as a scientist and had to be taken seriously. Samuel Wilberforce disagreed with the argument in the Quarterly Review in 1860. Human origins became central to the debate, although Darwin had avoided the subject in the book, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-henry-huxley">Thomas Henry Huxley</a> argued it strongly. Huxley used the debate to promote scientific naturalism over natural theology, and the X Club shaped much late-Victorian science.</p><p>By 1875 most scientists accepted that evolution occurred, but few thought natural selection significant. Objections included William Thomson's calculation that the Sun was only about 100 million years old and Fleeming Jenkin's argument that blending inheritance would prevent useful traits from accumulating. From the 1880s to the 1930s, in what is called the eclipse of Darwinism, saltationism, orthogenesis and neo-Lamarckism were given more credit. Religious responses ranged widely. Charles Kingsley saw natural selection as a noble conception of the Deity, Asa Gray defended the book in terms of theistic evolution, and Charles Hodge argued in 1874 that Darwinism, defined as rejecting design, was atheism. Political commentators also drew on the book, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer">Herbert Spencer</a>, whom Darwin credited with the phrase survival of the fittest.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;On the Origin of Species&#8221; cites where it covers Reception, Religious attitudes, Modern influence:</p><ul><li>Peter J. Bowler, Evolution: The History of an Idea (University of California Press, 2003)</li><li>Charles Darwin, The Annotated Origin: A Facsimile of the First Edition of On the Origin of Species Annotated by James T. Costa (Belknap Press of Harvard University Press, 2009)</li><li>Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection, or the Preservation of Favoured Races in the Struggle for Life (John Murray, 1860)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Darwin revised the text through six editions. The second, of 3,000 copies, appeared on 7 January 1860 with a new epigraph from Charles Kingsley and the phrase by the Creator in the last sentence. The third edition of 1861 added An Historical Sketch of the Recent Progress of Opinion on the Origin of Species, in which Darwin acknowledged that Patrick Matthew had anticipated natural selection in 1831. The fifth edition of 10 February 1869 first used survival of the fittest. The sixth, of 19 February 1872, answered George Jackson Mivart's objections in a new chapter, used the word evolution for the first time, dropped On from the title and was priced at 7s 6d. Darwin had been told of working men in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> clubbing together to buy the fifth edition.</p><p>Translations spread the book, but some carried their translators' own ideas. Heinrich Georg Bronn's German version of 1860 added essays and rendered favoured races as perfected races, and Clémence Royer's French version of 1862 added an introduction and notes that troubled Darwin. He remained unsatisfied until Edmond Barbier's translation of 1876. By Darwin's death in 1882 the book was in its sixth edition. A variorum recording every change between the editions was first published in 1959 and a concordance in 1981.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;On the Origin of Species&#8221; cites where it covers Events leading to publication: &quot;big book&quot; manuscript, Publication and subsequent editions:</p><ul><li>Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection, or the Preservation of Favoured Races in the Struggle for Life (John Murray, 1861)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The book legitimised scientific discussion of evolutionary mechanisms, and the new term Darwinism was used for the whole range of evolutionism. It was given more popular attention than almost any other scientific work. With the modern evolutionary synthesis of the 1930s and 1940s, when R. A. Fisher, Sewall Wright and J. B. S. Haldane merged Darwinian selection with a statistical understanding of Mendelian genetics, natural selection became central to evolutionary theory. Darwin's theory, with its tree-like model of branching common descent, has become the unifying theory of the life sciences, and it has become essential to applied sciences such as medicine and agriculture.</p><p>Darwin followed the book with The Variation of Animals and Plants Under Domestication in 1868 and The Descent of Man, and Selection in Relation to Sex in 1871, in which he published his own views on human evolution. Scholars have produced an extensive literature on his life and work, called the Darwin Industry. The book also lies behind a religion-based political controversy over how evolution is taught in schools, especially in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, where the American creation–evolution controversy began in the 1920s.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;On the Origin of Species&#8221; cites where it covers Impact on the scientific community, Impact on economic and political debates, Modern influence:</p><ul><li>Peter J. Bowler, Evolution: The History of an Idea (University of California Press, 2003)</li><li>Charles Darwin, The Annotated Origin: A Facsimile of the First Edition of On the Origin of Species Annotated by James T. Costa (Belknap Press of Harvard University Press, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On the Origin of Species is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>'s book of 1859 arguing that species arose by common descent through natural selection. Written as an abstract after Wallace's essay reached him, it went through six editions and became the foundation of evolutionary biology.</em></p>

